/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Sliding Window
Difficulty : Medium

Problem:
Given a string `s`, find the length of the longest
substring that contains no repeating characters.

Approach:
A brute-force approach checks every possible substring and
verifies whether it contains duplicate characters, resulting
in O(n²) or higher time complexity.

To optimize this, we use the Sliding Window technique along
with an array to store the last index at which each character
appeared.

As we traverse the string, we maintain a window containing
only unique characters. If the current character already
exists within the current window, we move the starting point
of the window just after its previous occurrence.

Why does this work?

The sliding window always represents a valid substring with
unique characters. Instead of restarting the search whenever
a duplicate is found, we simply adjust the left boundary of
the window. Since every character is processed only once,
the algorithm efficiently finds the maximum window length
without performing unnecessary comparisons.

Algorithm:
1. Initialize the starting index of the window.
2. Store the last occurrence of every character.
3. Traverse the string from left to right.
4. If the current character exists inside the current window,
   move the starting index after its previous occurrence.
5. Update the character's latest index.
6. Calculate the current window length.
7. Update the maximum length found.

Time Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
Each character is visited exactly once while the window moves
forward through the string.

Space Complexity : O(1)

Reason:
An array of fixed size (256) is used to store the last
occurrence of each ASCII character.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:
    int lengthOfLongestSubstring(string s) {
        int maxLength = 0;

        // Marks the index before the start of the current window.
        int windowStart = -1;

        // Stores the last occurrence of every ASCII character.
        vector<int> lastIndex(256, -1);

        // Traverse the string using the sliding window technique.
        for (int i = 0; i < s.size(); i++) {

            // If the current character already exists inside the
            // current window, move the window start forward.
            if (lastIndex[s[i]] > windowStart) {
                windowStart = lastIndex[s[i]];
            }

            // Update the latest occurrence of the current character.
            lastIndex[s[i]] = i;

            // Update the maximum length of a valid substring.
            maxLength = max(maxLength, i - windowStart);
        }

        return maxLength;
    }
};
